# Design constraints

## One routing policy

Attachment hops, Auto vision/files, and retry after a provider failure all go through `fallbackController.ts`. `chatService` / `prepareTurn` and `AIProviderManager` must call that module, not invent a second hop. After a vendor 401/403, do not bounce the same turn back onto that provider — move to the next distinct provider that can serve the payload.

## Honest names

A file named for Groq must not own Gemini/Cloudflare/OpenAI body builders. OpenAI-compatible request bodies live in `compatibleChatBody.ts`.

## Layer direction

AI normalizers must not import storage. Extract PDF/DOCX in `fileService` / chat materialize, then pass text or native parts in. `AIProviderManager` may trim context; it should not own identity or document unzipping.

## Split along existing seams

When touching chat: extract shared `prepareTurn` rather than copying Auto/tools/Flux/attachments into send, edit, and regenerate.
When touching ChatPage: extract `useChatStream`, `useChatAttachments`, or `useChatVoice` instead of more `useState` on the page.
When touching `api.ts`: add a domain module (`api/chat.ts`) rather than growing the 600+ line client.

## Multi-Agents

`Multi-Agents/` is a separate workspace runner, not the Express chat path. Do not route Ken user turns through it.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — second attachment router inside the manager
if (need === "files") hopToGemini();

// ✅ GOOD — one policy, extract if no native files model
const route = pickMultimodalRoute(models, requested, need) ?? requested;
```
